{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":1984963,"ns":0,"title":"Gelfond's constant","revisions":[{"revid":1376686890,"parentid":1372410667,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-09-25T17:38:48Z","size":14678,"sha1":"7fbc4311fb35eeca043e00366314d68566646b88","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Constant e raised to the power of pi}}\n{{More citations needed|date=October 2022}}\nIn [[mathematics]], the '''exponential of pi''' {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}},<ref>{{Cite web |title=A039661 - OEIS |url=https://oeis.org/A039661 |access-date=2024-10-27 |website=oeis.org}}</ref> also called '''Gelfond's constant''',<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weisstein |first=Eric W. |title=Gelfond's Constant |url=https://mathworld.wolfram.com/GelfondsConstant.html |access-date=2024-10-27 |website=Wolfram MathWorld |language=en}}</ref> is the real number {{mvar|[[e (mathematical constant)|e]]}} raised to the power [[Pi|{{pi}}]] (i.e., the value of the [[exponential function]] at {{pi}}).\n\nIts decimal expansion is given by\n: ''{{mvar|e{{sup|π}}}}'' = {{val|23.14069263277926900572}}... {{OEIS|A039661}}.\n\nLike both {{mvar|e}} and {{pi}}, this constant is both [[irrational number|irrational]] and [[transcendental number|transcendental]]. This follows from the [[Gelfond–Schneider theorem]], which establishes {{math|''a''{{sup|''b''}}}} to be transcendental, given that {{math|''a''}} is [[Algebraic number|algebraic]] and not equal to [[0|zero]] or [[1|one]] and {{math|''b''}} is algebraic but not [[Rational number|rational]]. We have\n<math display=\"block\">\n e^\\pi = (e^{i\\pi})^{-i} = (-1)^{-i},\n</math>\nwhere {{mvar|i}} is the [[imaginary unit]]. Since {{math|&minus;''i''}} is algebraic but not rational, {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}} is transcendental. The numbers {{pi}}, {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}}, and the [[lemniscate constant]] are also known to be [[Algebraic independence|algebraically independent]] over the [[rational number]]s, as demonstrated by [[Yuri Nesterenko (mathematician)|Yuri Nesterenko]].<ref>{{cite journal |author=Nesterenko, Y. |author-link=Yuri Valentinovich Nesterenko |year=1996 |title=Modular Functions and Transcendence Problems |journal=[[Comptes rendus de l'Académie des sciences|Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences, Série I]] |volume=322 |issue=10 |pages=909–914 |zbl=0859.11047}}</ref> It is not known whether {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}} is a [[Liouville number]].<ref name=\":7\">{{cite arXiv |eprint=math/0312440 |first=Michel |last=Waldschmidt |title=Open Diophantine Problems |date=2004-01-24}}</ref> The constant was mentioned in [[Hilbert's seventh problem]] alongside the [[Gelfond–Schneider constant]] {{math|2{{sup|{{sqrt|2}}}}}}, and the name \"Gelfond's constant\" stems from Soviet mathematician [[Alexander Gelfond]].<ref name=\"tijdeman\">{{cite book |last=Tijdeman |first=Robert |author-link=Robert Tijdeman |title=Mathematical Developments Arising from Hilbert Problems |publisher=[[American Mathematical Society]] |year=1976 |isbn=0-8218-1428-1 |editor=Felix E. Browder |editor-link=Felix Browder |series=[[Proceedings of Symposia in Pure Mathematics]] |volume=XXVIII.1 |pages=241–268 |chapter=On the Gel'fond–Baker method and its applications |zbl=0341.10026}}</ref>\n\n==Occurrences==\nThe constant {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}} appears in relation to the [[volume]]s of [[N-sphere|hyperspheres]].\n\n[[File:hypersphere_volume_and_surface_area_graphs.svg|thumb|Graphs of volumes ({{tmath|V_n}}) and [[surface area]]s ({{tmath|S_{n-1} }}) of {{mvar|n}}-balls of radius {{math|1}}]]\n\nThe volume of an ''n-sphere'' with [[radius]] {{math|''R''}} is given by\n<math display=\"block\">\n V_n(R) = \\frac{\\pi^\\frac{n}{2}R^n}{\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{n}{2} + 1\\right)},\n</math>\nwhere {{math|Γ}} is the [[gamma function]]. Considering only unit spheres ({{math|''R'' {{=}} 1}}) yields\n<math display=\"block\">\n V_n(1) = \\frac{\\pi^\\frac{n}{2}}{\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{n}{2} + 1\\right)},\n</math>\n\nAny even-dimensional 2''n''-sphere now gives\n<math display=\"block\">\n V_{2n}(1) = \\frac{\\pi^n}{\\Gamma(n + 1)} = \\frac{\\pi^n}{n!}.\n</math>\nSumming up all even-dimensional unit sphere volumes and utilizing the [[series expansion]] of the [[exponential function]] gives<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-05-26 |title=Sums of volumes of unit spheres |url=https://www.johndcook.com/blog/2019/05/26/sums-of-volumes-of-spheres/ |access-date=2024-10-27 |website=www.johndcook.com |language=en-US}}</ref>\n<math display=\"block\">\n \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty V_{2n}(1) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{\\pi^n}{n!} = \\exp(\\pi) = e^\\pi.\n</math>\n\nAlso, if one defines {{math|''k''{{sub|0}} {{=}} {{sfrac|1|{{sqrt|2}}}}}} and\n<math display=\"block\">\n k_{n+1} = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{1 - k_n^2}}{1 + \\sqrt{1 - k_n^2}}\n</math>\nfor {{math|''n'' > 0}}, then the sequence\n<math display=\"block\">\n (4/k_{n+1})^{2^{-n}}\n</math>\nconverges rapidly to {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}}.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Borwein |first1=J. |author1-link=Jonathan Borwein |url=https://archive.org/details/mathematicsbyexp00borw |title=Mathematics by Experiment: Plausible Reasoning in the 21st Century |last2=Bailey |first2=D. |publisher=A K Peters |year=2004 |isbn=1-56881-211-6 |place=Wellesley, MA |page=[https://archive.org/details/mathematicsbyexp00borw/page/n150 137] |zbl=1083.00001 |url-access=limited}}</ref>\n\n===In physics===\nThe [[Q factor]] is a measure of how [[underdamped]] an oscillating system is; at large values, it is approximately equal to the number of oscillation cycles it takes for the amplitude of the oscillator to decrease by a factor of {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}}, or the energy by a factor of {{math|''e''{{sup|2''π''}}}}.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Foundations of analog & digital electronic circuits|last=Anant.|first=Agarwal|date=2005|publisher=Elsevier|others=Lang, Jeffrey (Jeffrey H.)|isbn=9781558607354|location=Amsterdam|pages=647|oclc=60245509}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title = Light and Matter | author = Benjamin Crowell | year = 2006 | url = http://www.lightandmatter.com/lm | url-status = live | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110519093054/http://lightandmatter.com/lm/ | archive-date = 2011-05-19 }}, Ch. 18</ref>\n\nIn a [[water wave]], water molecules near the surface move in a circular motion as the wave propagates, attenuating exponentially with depth. At the characteristic length scale equal to {{sfrac|1|2}} the wavelength, known as the [[wave base]], the circular motion is attenuated by a factor of approximately {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}} relative to the surface and is negligible at lower depths. The circular motion leaves characteristic ripples in coastal seabeds shallower than the typical wave base.<ref name=\"mechanics\">{{cite book | title=Water wave mechanics for engineers and scientists | author=R. G. Dean | author2=R. A. Dalrymple | name-list-style=amp | year=1991 | series=Advanced Series on Ocean Engineering | volume=2 | publisher=World Scientific, Singapore | isbn=978-981-02-0420-4 }}</ref>\n\nA [[black hole]] bends light coming from behind it, producing an infinite series of lensed images corresponding to the number of times light wraps around the [[photon sphere]]. For a non-rotating black hole, the ratio of distances between successive images is asymptotically equal to {{math|''e''{{sup|−2''π''}}}}.<ref name=Sneppen>{{cite journal |last1=Sneppen |first1=Albert |title=Divergent reflections around the photon sphere of a black hole |journal=Scientific Reports |date=December 2021 |volume=11 |issue=1 |article-number=14247 |pmid=34244573| doi=10.1038/s41598-021-93595-w | doi-access=free|publisher=[[Cosmic Dawn Center]]|pmc=8270963 |bibcode=2021NatSR..1114247S }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Sutter |first1=Paul |title=Black holes warp the universe into a grotesque hall of mirrors |url=https://www.livescience.com/black-hole-mirror-copies.html |website=livescience.com |language=en |date=22 July 2021}}</ref>\n\nBlumhofer and Hutter (1997) describe a toy model of a quantum field theory with a [[Gribov ambiguity]]-like mechanism that produces multiple [[generation (particle physics)|generations]] of fermions, analogous to the real generations in the [[Standard Model]]. In the typical case, the mass ratio between successive generations is approximately {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}}.<ref name=Blumhofer-Hutter-1997->\n{{cite journal\n | first1 = A.  |last1 = Blumhofer\n | first2 = M.  |last2 = Hutter\n | year = 1997\n | title = Family structure from periodic solutions of an improved gap equation\n | journal = [[Nuclear Physics B]]\n | volume =  484   | issue = 1   | pages = 80–96\n | doi = 10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00644-X\n | bibcode = 1997NuPhB.484...80B\n }} {{erratum |doi=10.1016/S0550-3213(97)00228-9 |checked=yes}}\n</ref>\n\n==Similar or related constants==\n\n=== Ramanujan's constant ===\nThe number {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''{{sqrt|163}}}}}} is known as [[Ramanujan's constant]]. Its decimal expansion is given by\n: {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''{{sqrt|163}}}}}} = {{val|262537412640768743.99999999999925007259}}... {{OEIS|A060295}},\nwhich turns out to be very close to the integer {{math|640320{{sup|3}} + 744}}. This is an application of [[Heegner number]]s, where 163 is the Heegner number in question. This number was discovered in 1859 by the mathematician [[Charles Hermite]].<ref>{{cite book\n  | last = Barrow\n  | first = John D\n  | title = The Constants of Nature\n  | publisher = Jonathan Cape\n  | page = 72\n  | year = 2002\n  | location = London\n  | isbn = 0-224-06135-6 }}\n</ref> In a 1975 [[April Fools' Day|April Fool]] article in ''[[Scientific American]]'' magazine,<ref>{{cite journal\n  | last = Gardner\n  | first = Martin\n  | title = Mathematical Games\n  | journal = Scientific American\n  | volume = 232\n  | issue = 4\n  | page = 127\n  | date = April 1975\n  | publisher = Scientific American, Inc | doi = 10.1038/scientificamerican0575-102\n | bibcode = 1975SciAm.232e.102G\n }}\n</ref> \"Mathematical Games\" columnist [[Martin Gardner]] made the hoax claim that the number was in fact an integer, and that the Indian mathematical genius [[Srinivasa Ramanujan]] had predicted it—hence its name. Ramanujan's constant is also a transcendental number.\n\nThe coincidental closeness, to within one [[trillion]]th of the number {{math|640320{{sup|3}} + 744}} is explained by [[complex multiplication]] and the [[Q-expansion|''q''-expansion]] of the [[j-invariant]], specifically:\n<math display=\"block\">\n j\\big((1 + \\sqrt{-163})/2\\big) = (-640\\,320)^3\n</math>\nand\n<math display=\"block\">\n (-640\\,320)^3 = -e^{\\pi \\sqrt{163}} + 744 + O\\left(e^{-\\pi \\sqrt{163}}\\right),\n</math>\nwhere the error term\n<math display=\"block\">\n O\\left(e^{-\\pi {\\sqrt {163}}}\\right) = -196\\,884/e^{\\pi \\sqrt{163}} \\approx -196\\,884/(640\\,320^3 + 744) \\approx -0.000\\,000\\,000\\,000\\,75,\n</math>\nwhich explains why {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''{{sqrt|163}}}}}} is {{val|0.00000000000075}} below {{math|640320{{sup|3}} + 744}}.\n\nFor more detail on this proof, consult the article on [[Heegner number]]s.\n\n=== The number {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}} − ''π''}} ===\nThe number {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}} − ''π''}} is also very close to an integer, its decimal expansion being given by\n: {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}} − ''π''}} = {{val|19.99909997918947576726}}... {{OEIS|A018938}}.\n\nThe explanation for this seemingly remarkable coincidence was given by A.&nbsp;Doman in September 2023 and is a result of a sum related to [[Jacobi theta functions]] as follows:\n<math display=\"block\">\n \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty (8\\pi k^2 - 2) e^{-\\pi k^2} = 1.\n</math>\nThe first term dominates, since the sum of the terms for <math>k \\geq 2</math> total <math>\\sim 0.0003436.</math> The sum can therefore be truncated to <math>(8\\pi - 2) e^{-\\pi} \\approx 1,</math> where solving for <math>e^\\pi</math> gives <math>e^\\pi \\approx 8\\pi - 2.</math> Rewriting the approximation for <math>e^\\pi</math> and using the approximation for <math>7\\pi \\approx 22</math> gives\n<math display=\"block\">\n e^\\pi \\approx \\pi + 7\\pi - 2 \\approx \\pi + 22 - 2 = \\pi + 20.\n</math>\nThus, rearranging terms gives <math>e^\\pi - \\pi \\approx 20.</math> Ironically, the crude approximation for <math>7\\pi</math> yields an additional order of magnitude of precision.<ref name=\"MathWorld\">[[Eric Weisstein]], [http://mathworld.wolfram.com/AlmostInteger.html \"Almost Integer\"] at [[MathWorld]].</ref>\n\n=== The number {{math|''π''{{sup|''e''}}}} ===\nThe decimal expansion of {{math|''π''{{sup|''e''}}}} is given by\n: <math>\\pi^e = </math> {{val|22.45915771836104547342}}... {{OEIS|A059850}}.\n\nIt is not known whether or not this number is transcendental. Note that, by [[Gelfond–Schneider theorem]], we can only infer definitively whether or not {{math|''a''{{sup|''b''}}}} is transcendental if {{mvar|a}} and {{mvar|b}} are algebraic ({{mvar|a}} and {{mvar|b}} are both considered [[complex number]]s).\n\nIn the case of {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}}, we are only able to prove this number transcendental due to properties of complex exponential forms and the above equivalency given to transform it into {{math|(−1){{sup|−''i''}}}}, allowing the application of Gelfond–Schneider theorem.\n\n{{math|''π''{{sup|''e''}}}} has no such equivalence, and hence, as both {{pi}} and {{mvar|e}} are transcendental, we cannot use the Gelfond–Schneider theorem to draw conclusions about the transcendence of {{math|''π''{{sup|''e''}}}}. However the currently unproven [[Schanuel's conjecture]] would imply its transcendence.<ref name=\":1\">{{Cite web |last=Waldschmidt |first=Michel |date=2021 |title=Schanuel's Conjecture: algebraic independence of transcendental numbers |url=https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~michel.waldschmidt/articles/pdf/SchanuelEn.pdf}}</ref>\n\n=== The number {{math|''i''{{sup|''i''}}}} ===\nUsing the [[principal value]] of the [[complex logarithm]],\n<math display=\"block\">\n i^i = (e^{i\\pi/2})^i = e^{-\\pi/2} = (e^\\pi)^{-1/2}.\n</math>\nThe decimal expansion of is given by\n: <math>i^i = </math> {{val|0.20787957635076190854}}... {{OEIS|A049006}}.\n\nIts transcendence follows directly from the transcendence of {{math|''e''{{sup|''π''}}}} and directly from the Gelfond–Schneider theorem.\n\n== See also ==\n* [[Transcendental number]]\n* [[Transcendental number theory]], the study of questions related to transcendental numbers\n* [[Euler's identity]]\n* [[Gelfond–Schneider constant]]\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n==Further reading==\n* [[Alan Baker (mathematician)|Alan Baker]] and [[Gisbert Wüstholz]], ''Logarithmic Forms and Diophantine Geometry'', New Mathematical Monographs '''9''', Cambridge University Press, 2007, {{ISBN|978-0-521-88268-2}}\n\n==External links==\n* [http://mathworld.wolfram.com/GelfondsConstant.html Gelfond's constant at ''MathWorld'']\n* [https://web.archive.org/web/20091027100754/http://www.geocities.com/timeparadox/Muntekim.htm  A new complex power tower identity for Gelfond's constant]\n* [http://mathworld.wolfram.com/AlmostInteger.html Almost Integer at ''MathWorld'']\n\n{{Irrational number}}\n\n[[Category:Mathematical constants]]\n[[Category:Real transcendental numbers]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-02T09:19:05Z","lastrevid":1376686890,"length":14678}]}}